  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\n- Strong to severe storms most likely through this evening\n\nAfter above average uncertainty over the last couple of days, it \nseems that the atmosphere is finally showing its hand more now. A \nline of strong to marginally severe storms has organized over \nWisconsin, and is starting to move over the lake just before 1 pm \nEDT. \n\nThis line of storms is associated with a MCV from earlier storms, \nthat is riding the instability gradient aloft ahead of a stalled out \nsfc front over Northern portions of Illinois and Indiana as of early \nthis afternoon. The favored area for the strongest storms with this \nline will be over the far SW corner of the forecast area toward Van \nBuren and Kalamazoo counties. This is where the best instability \nresides over the area where 2000 J/kg of MUCAPE sneaks up. Deep \nlayer effective shear is also sufficient for some organized storms\nsitting at around 30 knots this afternoon.\n\nMid level lapse rates and CAPE thicknesses are not too exciting for \nlarge hail potential. Gusty winds look like the biggest threat with \nunidirectional wind profiles and the linear nature of the line. \nAreas across the far SW corner of the forecast area do have a little \nbetter chance of an isolated tornado being closer to the warm front \nand the better low level wind shear.  \n\nWhat boundaries that are left behind from this afternoon are likely \nto dictate the evolution of the storms for tonight. The reason this \nis important is that the larger scale model solutions all have a low \nlevel jet ramping up to our SW by mid evening, helping to fire more \nconvection along the stalled out front/outflow boundary. We are \nexpecting that most of any severe threat with the next round of \nstorms will likely stay south of our forecast area. \n\nWe do remain concerned however that heavy rainfall will remain \npossible. There are signals in the various model solutions and \nsuites that there could end up being a heavier localized band \nsomewhere over our far southern counties, or potentially into \nNorthern Indiana. If the confidence goes up it will be in our area, \nwe may try to issue a Flood Watch.\n\n- Residual small shower/storm chances Monday through Wednesday\n\nWe do expect that most of the widespread convection after anything \ntonight will likely stay farther south across Indiana and Ohio. That \nis where the effective front and baroclinic boundary ends up. We \ndo not see the degree of heat or instability south of the \nboundary, but enough moisture and weak short waves in the steady \nmid level flow will keep shower/storm chances present for the area\nfor mainly afternoon and evenings Monday through Wednesday. Lake \nbreezes and any other mesoscale boundaries will be the main focus \nfor any diurnal development.\n\n- Mainly dry and seasonable Thursday and into next weekend\n\nThe models have been showing that there is a good chance we dry out \nfor at least Thursday and Friday, and maybe even into next weekend. \nWe will see a upper ridge across Canada to our NW, that will support \nsfc ridging to our north. This will bring in a drier air mass from \nCanada with seasonable temperatures. \n\nEventually a SW flow aloft tries to build over the country, which \nwould push the warmer air mass toward the area, and maybe \nconvection along the periphery around it. There is too much \nuncertainty at this time to try and get too detailed with that at \nthis time.",
